    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently assisting with the acquisition of a new collection of 19th-century manuscripts for our university archives here in Belgium. We are trying to decide whether it would be more effective to use the Dewey Decimal System or the Library of Congress Classification for this specific set of materials. Could you provide a comparison of the two systems, specifically regarding their metadata capabilities and how they handle granular organization for historical manuscripts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Library of Congress Classification typically offers superior granularity for specific historical manuscripts due to its alphabetically structured call numbers that can accommodate unique identifiers for individual works. In contrast, the Dewey Decimal System often groups materials into broader subject ranges, which may limit the ability to distinguish fine details within niche 19th-century archives. While both systems support basic metadata entry, LC's third-level designations are generally more flexible for complex historical collections found in specialized institutions like Belgian university libraries.",
