    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. My main concern is how we manage the actual physical intake process. When items are being moved from the temporary storage into the new system, things inevitably get a bit messy—items get misplaced, or the initial sorting becomes really chaotic before the permanent labels are applied. In your experience, does one system handle that initial period of organizational disorder better than the other to prevent loss or misplacement?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Library of Congress Classification generally handles physical disorganization better because many modern implementation strategies utilize temporary \"slop file\" bins before assigning permanent, granular labels. Since the Dewey Decimal System relies on broad numerical groupings, it can become inefficient in these chaotic phases, making it harder to track individual fragile manuscripts without losing their context. Choosing the system that aligns with a robust temporary holding strategy is more critical than the system's long-term organizational capabilities for surviving that initial messy intake period.",
